WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.002 --> 00:00:07.007
"Yerba Mate and Cancer:
Do Its Benefits Outweigh Its Risks?"

00:00:07.106 --> 00:00:09.840
Yerba mate teas have been
consumed for centuries

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.240
by the South American
indigenous peoples,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.560
and today, certain regions
of Latin America

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:15.760
have a quart (950 ml) a day.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.040
So, understanding
what's in mate is important,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.080
particularly if there are
"High levels of carcinogenic

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.720
polycyclic aromatic
hydrocarbons,"

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.280
including one of the most
potent, benzopyrene,

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.080
which is known to be
cancer-causing in humans

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.560
and is found concentrated
in cigarette smoke,

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.800
grilled and barbequed meat,

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.480
and mate.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.920
These carcinogenic compounds
are fat soluble, though,

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.480
and may not readily transfer
from the mate leaves

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.680
into the water.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.320
It turns out transfer rates
can be anywhere

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.400
between 1 percent
to 50 percent,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.080
depending on
the brewing method.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.160
But levels in mate leaves
are so high

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.000
that even if about five percent
transfers into the tea,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.680
heavy mate consumption could be
the benzopyrene equivalent

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.680
to smoking
about four cigarettes.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.360
Each cigarette has
around 10 nanograms,

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.760
so that would be,
like, 40 nanograms,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.240
or the amount
of carcinogenic benzopyrene

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.720
found in about one-tenth
of a serving

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.160
of grilled chicken.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.400
Okay, but a direct comparison

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.920
between breathing in
the chemical

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.320
versus eating or drinking it
may be inaccurate

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.560
because we absorb it
differently.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.200
Maybe, but when the levels
of a breakdown product

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.600
of these kinds of chemicals
was measured in the urine

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.800
of smokers
versus mate drinkers,

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.960
those who drank more than two
or four cups (480 or 960 ml)

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.320
of mate a day

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.040
appeared to have
as much of these carcinogens

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.800
flowing through their systems
as smokers.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.800
Okay, but does this
actually translate out

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.840
into higher cancer rates?

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.480
I mean, mate has
more antioxidant power

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:45.440
than green tea,

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.080
significantly increasing
the antioxidant capacity

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:48.880
of the bloodstreams

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.720
of those randomized
to drink it.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.000
So, might the good
outweigh the bad?

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:54.960
Apparently not.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.600
Mate drinkers have
about double the odds of cancers

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.784
of the mouth, throat,
esophagus, and voicebox.

